// @check
// @error no `n` on `T` — `T` is a type parameter with no bound, and a parameter's members come from its bound. Declare one: `<T: { n: … }>`
// AN UNBOUNDED `<T>` KEEPS ITS OWN PATH, and this is what that path SAYS. The
// bounded sibling is `error-strict-body-member-not-in-bound.vl`, which can name the
// bound and everything it grants; an unbounded parameter has nothing to name, so
// the sentence is about the DECLARATION and the one edit that fixes it.
//
// D1004 / D1221 — until this landed the whole diagnostic was `[ERROR]:`, a caret and
// no words: `memberFloorMsg`'s `TyVar` rung answered "" and `memberFloorErr` raised
// it. "" is a sentinel meaning "fine, or deferred" to the two constraint-pin callers,
// and neither can receive a `TyVar` (both guard on `!tyHasHole`).
//
// THE RULE THE SENTENCE STATES: a generic body is checked ONCE, so `x.n` needs one
// type and only the declaration supplies it. `<T: { n: i32 }>` is what makes this
// program run — measured, at two instantiation shapes. Whether an unbounded `<T>`
// should instead INFER that bound is the owner question on ROADMAP.
function getN<T>(x: T): i32 {
  x.n
}

print(getN({ n: 1 }))
